# General Script Development Rules

These are general script development rules that apply across all projects.

---

## 🚨🚨🚨 CRITICAL: NEVER Use `| head` or `| tail -N` in Command Pipes 🚨🚨🚨

**⚠️ THIS IS THE #1 MISTAKE AI ASSISTANTS MAKE - READ THIS FIRST ⚠️**

## 🚨 CRITICAL: NEVER Use While Loops That Wait for Processes

**⚠️ NEVER use `while` loops that wait for processes to complete - they can hang indefinitely and block the terminal.**

**❌ FORBIDDEN (will hang indefinitely):**
```bash
# ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Hangs if process never completes or log never updates
while ps aux | grep -q "[v]alidate_data"; do 
    sleep 5
    tail -5 /tmp/log.log
done

# ❌ NEVER DO THIS - Can hang if process is stuck
while [ -f /tmp/pidfile ]; do
    sleep 1
    tail -10 /tmp/log.log
done
```

**✅ ALWAYS DO THIS INSTEAD:**
```bash
# ✅ Option 1: Use timeout to prevent infinite wait
timeout 300 bash -c 'while ps aux | grep -q "[v]alidate_data"; do sleep 5; tail -5 /tmp/log.log; done' || echo "Timeout or process completed"

# ✅ Option 2: Check process status separately, then view log
PID=$(pgrep -f validate_data)
if [ -n "$PID" ]; then
    echo "Process still running (PID: $PID)"
    tail -20 /tmp/log.log
else
    echo "Process completed"
    tail -100 /tmp/log.log
fi

# ✅ Option 3: Use background monitoring with explicit timeout
bazel run //:script > /tmp/script.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID"
# Check periodically with timeout
for i in {1..60}; do  # Max 5 minutes (60 * 5s)
    sleep 5
    if ! ps -p $PID > /dev/null; then
        echo "Process completed"
        tail -100 /tmp/script.log
        break
    fi
    tail -10 /tmp/script.log
done
```

**Why this matters:**
- While loops without timeouts can hang indefinitely
- If process is stuck, loop never exits
- Terminal becomes unresponsive
- Can't interrupt or debug easily
- AI assistants can't send Ctrl+C to break the loop

**When to use while loops:**
- ✅ Only with explicit timeout (`timeout` command or counter)
- ✅ Only with break conditions that are guaranteed to occur
- ✅ Only when you can interrupt with Ctrl+C (not in automated scripts)

**Rationale:**
- Prevents terminal from hanging indefinitely
- Allows early detection of stuck processes
- Makes monitoring predictable and interruptible
- Follows best practices for process monitoring

**BEFORE running ANY command, check if it contains `| head` or `| tail -N` in a pipe. If it does, STOP and use the background logging pattern instead.**

**Common violations to watch for:**
- `command 2>&1 | head -N` ❌
- `command 2>&1 | tail -N` ❌
- `command 2>&1 | grep "pattern" | head -N` ❌
- `bazel run //:script 2>&1 | grep -E "pattern" -A 15 | head -40` ❌

**If you see ANY of these patterns, you MUST use the background logging pattern instead.**

**🚨 THIS IS THE #1 MISTAKE AI ASSISTANTS MAKE 🚨**

**NEVER, EVER use `| head`, `| tail -N`, or `| grep | head` in command pipes. This rule applies to ALL commands, not just long-running scripts.**

**❌ FORBIDDEN (will block monitoring):**
```bash
# ❌ NEVER DO THIS - EVER
bazel run //:script 2>&1 | head -50
bazel run //:script 2>&1 | tail -15
bazel run //:script 2>&1 | grep "pattern" | head -20
command | head -N  # ANY use of head in pipes
command | tail -N   # ANY use of tail -N in pipes (use tail -f on log files instead)

# ❌ SPECIFIC FORBIDDEN PATTERN (common violation):
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data -- --check-import-completeness-by-file 2>&1 | grep -E "pattern" -A 15 | head -40
```

**✅ ALWAYS DO THIS INSTEAD:**
```bash
# ✅ Run in background, then view logs
bazel run //:script > /tmp/script.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID"
tail -f /tmp/script.log                    # Real-time monitoring
tail -50 /tmp/script.log                   # View last 50 lines (on log file, not in pipe)
tail -f /tmp/script.log | grep "pattern"   # Filter while monitoring (on log file)

# ✅ CORRECT PATTERN for the forbidden example above:
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data -- --check-import-completeness-by-file > /tmp/validate.log 2>&1 &
tail -f /tmp/validate.log | grep -E "Top file discrepancies|Per-file comparison|files with significant" -A 15
# Or view last 40 lines after completion:
tail -40 /tmp/validate.log
```

**Why this is critical:**
- `| head` or `| tail -N` in pipes blocks monitoring and prevents seeing progress
- You can't tell if script is stuck or just slow
- Progress messages are hidden
- Pipe closes early, may cause SIGPIPE errors
- **AI assistants CONSTANTLY violate this** - check EVERY command for `| head` or `| tail -N`

**If you need to see output:**
1. Run command in background: `command > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &`
2. Monitor: `tail -f /tmp/log.log`
3. View last N lines: `tail -N /tmp/log.log` (on the log file, NOT in the pipe)

## Script Naming Conventions

**Scripts that evaluate, analyze, or test should NOT be named `test_*.py` to avoid confusion with unit tests.**

**✅ GOOD - Descriptive script names:**
```python
# Scripts that evaluate/analyze
evaluate_clustering_threshold.py
analyze_performance.py
check_data_quality.py

# Unit tests (in tests/ directory)
test_clustering_evaluator.py
test_performance.py
test_data_quality.py
```

**❌ BAD - Confusing names:**
```python
# ❌ Looks like a unit test but is actually a script
test_performance.py  # Should be: analyze_performance.py
test_data_quality.py  # Should be: evaluate_data_quality.py
```

**Naming patterns:**
- **Evaluation scripts:** `evaluate_*.py`, `check_*.py`, `analyze_*.py`
- **Unit tests:** `test_*.py` (always in `tests/` directory)
- **Utility scripts:** `fix_*.py`, `import_*.py`, `export_*.py`

**Rationale:**
- Prevents confusion between scripts and tests
- Makes it clear what files are runnable scripts vs test code
- Follows Python convention (test files are for pytest/unittest)
- Easier to find and understand project structure

## Debugging and Script Development

**ALWAYS use Bazel for project-specific debugging tools - never run Python directly.**

**✅ GOOD - Use Bazel from project root:**
```bash
# Always run from project root directory
cd /path/to/project
bazel run //:explore_db -- --query "SELECT ..."
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data
bazel run //scripts/salary:collect_dol_golden_test_data -- --limit-files 5
```

**❌ BAD - Direct Python:**
```bash
python3 -c "import django; ..."  # Fails: ModuleNotFoundError
python explore_db.py  # Fails: Django not in system Python
```

**❌ BAD - Running Bazel from wrong directory:**
```bash
cd data/salary/dol_data  # ❌ Wrong directory
bazel run //scripts/salary:collect_dol_golden_test_data  # May fail or behave unexpectedly
```

**CRITICAL: Always run Bazel commands from the project root:**
- Bazel needs to find the `WORKSPACE` or `MODULE.bazel` file in the root
- Relative paths in scripts assume project root as working directory
- Bazel's sandbox and runfiles work correctly only from root

**Why:**
- Project dependencies (Django, etc.) are only available in Bazel environment
- System Python doesn't have project libraries
- Bazel ensures correct environment and dependencies
- Running from root ensures correct path resolution and workspace detection

## Script Development Flow

When debugging and need a script:

1. **Check documentation** - Can you reuse an existing tool?
2. **Check if trivial** - Is it just command-line tools (`grep`, `curl`, `ls`)?
3. **Check existing scripts** - Search codebase for similar functionality before creating new scripts
4. **Check recent history** - Have you created similar scripts recently?
   - **If yes** → Create/update a **permanent script** with logging
   - **If no** → Create a **throwaway script** (but still log it)
5. **ALWAYS ask for explicit confirmation before creating new scripts** - Only create new scripts when user explicitly requests it
6. **Always use Bazel** for project-specific libraries
7. **Log all script usage:**
   - Permanent scripts: Log each call to their own log file
   - Throwaway scripts: Log to shared throwaway calls log with context

## Rule: Check Recent History and Logs Before Re-Running Scripts

**ALWAYS check recent conversation history and existing log files before re-running scripts.**

**Before running any script:**
1. ✅ **Check conversation history** - Was this script just run? What were the results?
2. ✅ **Check for existing log files** - Look for recent logs in `/tmp/` or project log directories
3. ✅ **Check log timestamps** - If log is recent (< 5 minutes), use it instead of re-running
4. ✅ **Only re-run if** - Log is stale, missing, or user explicitly requests re-run

**✅ GOOD - Check logs first:**
```bash
# Check if recent log exists
if [ -f /tmp/validate_tables.log ] && [ $(find /tmp/validate_tables.log -mmin -5) ]; then
    echo "Using recent log file"
    cat /tmp/validate_tables.log | tail -200
else
    echo "Log is stale or missing, running script..."
    bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data > /tmp/validate_tables.log 2>&1 &
fi
```

**❌ BAD - Re-run without checking:**
```bash
# Don't immediately re-run if script was just executed
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data > /tmp/validate_tables.log 2>&1 &
# Should check if log exists and is recent first
```

**When to check logs:**
- ✅ Script was run in recent conversation history
- ✅ Long-running scripts that take > 30 seconds
- ✅ Validation or analysis scripts that produce reports
- ✅ Any script that writes to `/tmp/` or log directories

**When re-running is OK:**
- ✅ User explicitly requests re-run
- ✅ Log is > 1 hour old
- ✅ Script parameters changed
- ✅ Code was modified since last run

**Rationale:**
- Prevents unnecessary re-execution of long-running scripts
- Saves time and resources
- Reduces redundant work
- Better user experience (faster results)

## Rule: Always Search for Existing Scripts Before Creating New Ones

**ALWAYS search for existing scripts with similar functionality before creating a new script.**

**Before creating a new script:**
1. ✅ **Search codebase** - Use `codebase_search` or `grep` to find similar functionality
2. ✅ **Check `scripts/README.md`** - Review categorized list of existing scripts
3. ✅ **Consider extending existing script** - Add new mode/flag to existing script rather than creating new one
4. ✅ **Only create new script if functionality is truly distinct** - Avoid duplication
5. ✅ **Get explicit user confirmation** - Only create new scripts when user explicitly requests it

**✅ GOOD - Extend existing script:**
```python
# Add new flag to existing validate_data.py
parser.add_argument(
    '--check-new-feature',
    action='store_true',
    help='Check new feature'
)
```

**❌ BAD - Create duplicate script:**
```python
# Don't create validate_data_v2.py when validate_data.py exists
# Instead, add flags to existing script
```

**When to create new script:**
- ✅ Functionality is fundamentally different (e.g., clustering vs validation)
- ✅ Script serves different purpose (e.g., fix vs validate)
- ✅ Script is in different category (e.g., deployment vs data processing)
- ✅ **User explicitly requests new script**

**When to extend existing script:**
- ✅ Same category (e.g., both validation scripts)
- ✅ Similar functionality (e.g., both check data quality)
- ✅ Can be controlled via flags/modes

**Rationale:**
- Prevents script proliferation and duplication
- Makes functionality discoverable in one place
- Reduces maintenance burden
- Follows DRY principle
- Easier for developers to find and use tools

## Rule: Always Generalize and Remove Duplicative Scripts

**ALWAYS try to generalize and remove duplicative scripts whenever possible.**

**Before keeping a script:**
1. ✅ **Check if functionality exists in master/comprehensive scripts** - Consolidate into unified scripts
2. ✅ **Check if logic can be extracted to shared modules** - Make scripts facades over testable modules
3. ✅ **Check if script is just a convenience wrapper** - Consider adding flags to master script instead
4. ✅ **Delete redundant scripts** - Don't keep multiple scripts doing the same thing

**When to delete a script:**
- ✅ Functionality is fully covered by a master/comprehensive script
- ✅ Script is just a thin wrapper around shared logic
- ✅ Script has misleading name or unclear purpose
- ✅ Script duplicates functionality available elsewhere
- ✅ Can be replaced by adding a flag to existing comprehensive script

**When to keep a script:**
- ✅ Script serves a distinct, non-overlapping purpose
- ✅ Script is in a different category (e.g., deployment vs validation)
- ✅ Script provides unique functionality not available elsewhere
- ✅ Script is actively used in workflows and migration would be disruptive

**Process for removing duplicative scripts:**
1. **Extract logic to shared module** (if not already done)
2. **Integrate into master script** (add flags/options if needed)
3. **Update all references** (docs, other scripts, BUILD files)
4. **Delete the script** and its BUILD target
5. **Update documentation** to point to master script

**✅ GOOD - Consolidated into master script:**
```python
# Master validation script with flags
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data -- --check-import-completeness-by-file
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data -- --check-import-completeness-by-year
```

**❌ BAD - Multiple scripts doing same thing:**
```python
# Don't keep separate scripts when master script covers it
bazel run //scripts/salary:check_per_year_discrepancies  # ❌ Redundant
bazel run //scripts/salary:check_file_discrepancies      # ❌ Redundant
bazel run //scripts/salary:validate_data                 # ✅ Master script
```

**Rationale:**
- Reduces maintenance burden (one script to maintain, not many)
- Prevents confusion (one place to find functionality)
- Makes functionality discoverable (all validation in one script)
- Follows DRY principle (don't repeat yourself)
- Easier to extend (add flags to master script, not create new scripts)

## Rule: Run Long-Running Scripts in Background and Monitor Logs

**ALWAYS run long-running or potentially slow scripts in background mode and monitor their progress through logs.**

**Why:**
- Prevents terminal from blocking indefinitely
- Allows early detection of stuck scripts
- Enables monitoring progress without blocking
- Better user experience (can continue working)

**CRITICAL: NEVER use `| head`, `| tail -N`, or `| grep | head` in command pipes - EVER!**

**This is a CRITICAL rule that AI assistants frequently violate. Always run commands in background and monitor logs instead.**

**❌ NEVER DO THIS (violates rule):**
- ❌ `bazel run //:script 2>&1 | head -50` - Blocks monitoring, hides progress
- ❌ `bazel run //:script 2>&1 | tail -15` - Can't see real-time progress  
- ❌ `bazel run //:script 2>&1 | grep "pattern" | head -20` - Blocks monitoring, hides progress
- ❌ `command | head -N` - ANY use of head in pipes is forbidden
- ❌ `command | tail -N` - ANY use of tail -N in pipes is forbidden (use `tail -f` on log files instead)

**✅ ALWAYS DO THIS INSTEAD:**
- ✅ Run in background: `bazel run //:script > /tmp/script.log 2>&1 &`
- ✅ Monitor logs: `tail -f /tmp/script.log`
- ✅ View last N lines: `tail -N /tmp/script.log` (on the log file, not in pipe)
- ✅ Filter while monitoring: `tail -f /tmp/script.log | grep "pattern"` (on log file, not command output)
- ✅ `bazel run //:script > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &` then `tail -f /tmp/log.log | grep "pattern"` - Monitorable

**✅ GOOD - Background execution with log monitoring:**
```bash
# Run in background, redirect output to log file
bazel run //:investigate_validation_issues > /tmp/validation_investigation.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Monitor progress by tailing logs
tail -f /tmp/validation_investigation.log

# Or check periodically
watch -n 2 'tail -20 /tmp/validation_investigation.log'

# Check if still running
ps -p $PID
```

**✅ GOOD - Using nohup for persistence:**
```bash
# Run with nohup (survives terminal close)
nohup bazel run //:investigate_validation_issues > /tmp/validation.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID, monitoring: tail -f /tmp/validation.log"
```

**✅ GOOD - Quick progress check:**
```bash
# Run in background
bazel run //:long_script > /tmp/script.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Wait a bit, then check progress
sleep 5
if ps -p $PID > /dev/null; then
  echo "Still running, last 10 lines:"
  tail -10 /tmp/script.log
else
  echo "Completed, exit code: $?"
  cat /tmp/script.log
fi
```

**✅ GOOD - Filtering output while preserving full log:**
```bash
# Run in background with full log
bazel run //:validate_data > /tmp/validate_data.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Monitor with filtering (full log still available)
tail -f /tmp/validate_data.log | grep -E "Running|Missing Salary|ERROR|WARNING"

# Or check full log anytime
tail -50 /tmp/validate_data.log
```

**❌ BAD - Foreground execution (blocks terminal):**
```bash
# Blocks terminal - can't monitor or interrupt easily
bazel run //:investigate_validation_issues
```

**❌ BAD - Piping through grep/head that prevents monitoring:**
```bash
# ❌ BAD: grep filters out progress messages, head closes pipe early
bazel run //:validate_data 2>&1 | grep -A 30 "Missing Salary" | head -50

# Problems:
# - Progress messages like "Running data sanity validation..." are hidden
# - head -50 closes pipe after 50 lines, may cause SIGPIPE
# - Can't see what stage script is at
# - Can't monitor progress in real-time
```

**✅ GOOD - Proper filtering approach:**
```bash
# Option 1: Run in background, filter when monitoring
bazel run //:validate_data > /tmp/validate.log 2>&1 &
tail -f /tmp/validate.log | grep -E "Missing Salary|DATA SANITY"

# Option 2: Use tee to see both filtered and full output
bazel run //:validate_data 2>&1 | tee /tmp/validate.log | grep -E "Missing Salary|DATA SANITY"
```

**When to use background:**
- ✅ Scripts that query databases (may be slow)
- ✅ Scripts that process large datasets
- ✅ Scripts that make network requests
- ✅ Scripts that iterate over many records
- ✅ Any script that might take > 10 seconds
- ✅ Validation or investigation scripts
- ✅ Scripts where you want to filter output (always log full output first)

**When foreground is OK:**
- ✅ Quick commands (< 5 seconds)
- ✅ Interactive scripts requiring input
- ✅ Scripts that need immediate output
- ✅ Simple queries or lookups

**Rule: Never Use grep/head/tail Filters in Pipes with Long-Running Commands**

**🚨 CRITICAL: This rule applies to ALL commands, not just long-running scripts. NEVER use `| head` or `| tail -N` in pipes. See the rule at the top of this file.**

**Why this matters:**
- `head -N` or `tail -N` in a pipe closes the pipe early, may cause SIGPIPE errors
- Progress messages are hidden (don't match grep pattern)
- Can't tell if script is stuck or just slow
- Can't see what stage script is at
- Can't monitor progress in real-time
- **AI assistants CONSTANTLY violate this rule** - this is the #1 mistake
- Commands with `| head` block and prevent monitoring
- You can't interrupt or see what's happening

**❌ BAD - Direct piping (violates rule):**
```bash
# ❌ NEVER do this for long-running scripts
bazel run //:script 2>&1 | tail -15
bazel run //:script 2>&1 | head -50
bazel run //:script 2>&1 | grep "pattern" | tail -20
```

**✅ GOOD - Background execution, then filter logs:**
```bash
# Run in background with full logging
bazel run //:script > /tmp/script.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID"

# Then monitor/filter the log file (not the pipe)
tail -f /tmp/script.log                    # Real-time monitoring
tail -15 /tmp/script.log                   # Last 15 lines
tail -f /tmp/script.log | grep "pattern"   # Filter while monitoring
```

**✅ GOOD - Always log full output, filter when viewing:**
```bash
# Log everything, filter when monitoring
bazel run //:validate_data > /tmp/validate.log 2>&1 &
tail -f /tmp/validate.log | grep "Missing Salary"  # Filter while monitoring
tail -50 /tmp/validate.log  # See full output anytime
```

**❌ BAD - Filtering in the command prevents monitoring:**
```bash
# Can't see progress, can't tell if stuck
bazel run //:validate_data 2>&1 | grep "Missing Salary" | head -50
```

**Monitoring strategies:**

1. **Real-time tail:**
   ```bash
   tail -f /tmp/script.log
   ```

2. **Periodic checks:**
   ```bash
   watch -n 2 'tail -20 /tmp/script.log && echo "---" && ps -p $PID'
   ```

3. **Progress indicators:**
   - Look for log messages showing progress (e.g., "Processing record 100/1000")
   - Check for increasing counts or percentages
   - Watch for error messages or warnings

4. **Stuck detection:**
   - If logs stop updating for > 2 minutes, script may be stuck
   - Check process status: `ps -p $PID`
   - Look for database locks or network timeouts in logs
   - Consider killing and investigating: `kill $PID`

**Log file naming convention:**
- Use `/tmp/<script_name>.log` for temporary logs
- Include timestamp if multiple runs: `/tmp/<script_name>_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).log`
- Use descriptive names: `/tmp/validation_investigation.log`

## Rule: Always Run Scripts in Monitorable Mode and Babysit for Early Course Correction

**CRITICAL: ALWAYS run scripts in monitorable mode (background with logs) and actively babysit them to detect and fix issues early.**

**Why this matters:**
- Early detection prevents wasted time (catch errors in first 5 minutes, not after 2 hours)
- Enables course correction before script processes too much data incorrectly
- Prevents silent failures or incorrect data processing
- Saves resources (CPU, database connections, disk I/O)
- Allows intervention before script completes with bad results

**✅ GOOD - Monitorable execution with active babysitting:**
```bash
# 1. Start script in background with full logging
bazel run //:process_data > /tmp/process_data.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID, monitoring: tail -f /tmp/process_data.log"

# 2. IMMEDIATELY check initial output (first 30 seconds)
sleep 5
echo "=== Initial check ==="
tail -20 /tmp/process_data.log

# 3. Monitor actively for first few minutes
for i in {1..6}; do
  sleep 10
  echo "=== Check $i (after $((i*10)) seconds) ==="
  tail -10 /tmp/process_data.log
  
  # Check for errors or warnings
  if grep -q "ERROR\|WARNING\|Exception\|Traceback" /tmp/process_data.log; then
    echo "⚠️  ERRORS DETECTED - Reviewing..."
    tail -50 /tmp/process_data.log | grep -A 10 -B 5 "ERROR\|WARNING\|Exception"
    read -p "Continue monitoring? (y/n) " -n 1 -r
    if [[ ! $REPLY =~ ^[Yy]$ ]]; then
      kill $PID
      echo "Stopped script. Review logs: tail -100 /tmp/process_data.log"
      exit 1
    fi
  fi
  
  # Check if still running
  if ! ps -p $PID > /dev/null; then
    echo "Script completed. Exit code: $?"
    tail -50 /tmp/process_data.log
    break
  fi
done

# 4. Continue monitoring periodically if still running
echo "Script still running. Monitor with: tail -f /tmp/process_data.log"
```

**✅ GOOD - Quick validation before letting script run:**
```bash
# Start script
bazel run //:import_data > /tmp/import.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Wait 30 seconds, then validate initial behavior
sleep 30
tail -30 /tmp/import.log

# Check for expected patterns
if ! grep -q "Processing\|Starting\|Loaded" /tmp/import.log; then
  echo "⚠️  Script may not be working correctly - no expected output"
  tail -50 /tmp/import.log
  kill $PID
  exit 1
fi

# Check for errors
if grep -q "ERROR\|Exception\|Failed" /tmp/import.log; then
  echo "❌ Errors detected early - stopping"
  tail -50 /tmp/import.log
  kill $PID
  exit 1
fi

echo "✅ Initial validation passed. Continue monitoring: tail -f /tmp/import.log"
```

**✅ GOOD - Monitor with progress validation:**
```bash
# Start script
bazel run //:cluster_employers > /tmp/cluster.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Monitor and validate progress
LAST_COUNT=0
STUCK_COUNT=0

while ps -p $PID > /dev/null; do
  sleep 15
  
  # Check for progress updates
  CURRENT_COUNT=$(grep -o "Processed [0-9]*" /tmp/cluster.log | tail -1 | grep -o "[0-9]*" || echo "0")
  
  if [ "$CURRENT_COUNT" = "$LAST_COUNT" ]; then
    STUCK_COUNT=$((STUCK_COUNT + 1))
    echo "⚠️  No progress for $((STUCK_COUNT * 15)) seconds"
    
    if [ $STUCK_COUNT -ge 4 ]; then
      echo "❌ Script appears stuck (no progress for 60+ seconds)"
      tail -50 /tmp/cluster.log
      kill $PID
      exit 1
    fi
  else
    STUCK_COUNT=0
    echo "✅ Progress: $CURRENT_COUNT records"
  fi
  
  LAST_COUNT=$CURRENT_COUNT
  
  # Check for errors
  if grep -q "ERROR\|Exception" /tmp/cluster.log; then
    echo "❌ Errors detected"
    tail -50 /tmp/cluster.log | grep -A 10 "ERROR\|Exception"
    kill $PID
    exit 1
  fi
done

echo "Script completed. Final output:"
tail -50 /tmp/cluster.log
```

**✅ GOOD - Slow exponential backoff for long-running scripts:**
```bash
# Start script
bazel run //:long_running_script > /tmp/script.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Monitor with slow exponential backoff (check frequently at first, less often over time)
INTERVAL=10  # Start with 10 seconds
ITERATION=0

while ps -p $PID > /dev/null; do
  sleep $INTERVAL
  ITERATION=$((ITERATION + 1))
  
  echo "=== Check $ITERATION (interval: ${INTERVAL}s) ==="
  tail -10 /tmp/script.log
  
  # Check for errors
  if grep -q "ERROR\|Exception" /tmp/script.log; then
    echo "❌ Errors detected"
    tail -50 /tmp/script.log | grep -A 10 "ERROR\|Exception"
    kill $PID
    exit 1
  fi
  
  # Slow exponential backoff: multiply by ~1.5 each iteration
  # Pattern: 10s -> 15s -> 23s -> 35s -> 53s -> 80s -> 120s -> 180s -> 270s -> 405s
  # Cap at 5 minutes (300s) to avoid too long between checks
  if [ $INTERVAL -lt 300 ]; then
    INTERVAL=$((INTERVAL * 3 / 2))  # Multiply by 1.5 (integer math)
  fi
done

echo "Script completed. Final output:"
tail -50 /tmp/script.log
```

**❌ BAD - Dramatic backoff (too aggressive):**
```bash
# ❌ BAD: Jumping from 10s to 300s to 900s is too dramatic
# Misses issues that occur in the intermediate time periods
INTERVAL=10
while ps -p $PID > /dev/null; do
  sleep $INTERVAL
  # ... check logs ...
  INTERVAL=$((INTERVAL * 30))  # ❌ Too aggressive: 10s -> 300s -> 9000s
done
```

**Rule: Use Slow Exponential Backoff for Monitoring Intervals**

**ALWAYS use slow exponential backoff (multiply by ~1.5) for monitoring intervals in long-running scripts.**

**Why:**
- Check frequently at first to catch early errors (first 5-10 minutes are critical)
- Gradually reduce check frequency as script proves stable
- Avoid dramatic jumps that miss issues in intermediate periods
- Balance between responsiveness and resource usage

**Backoff pattern:**
- ✅ **Good:** 10s → 15s → 23s → 35s → 53s → 80s → 120s → 180s → 270s → 405s
- ❌ **Bad:** 10s → 300s → 900s (too dramatic, misses intermediate issues)

**Implementation:**
- Start with 10-15 seconds for first few checks
- Multiply interval by ~1.5 each iteration (use integer math: `INTERVAL=$((INTERVAL * 3 / 2))`)
- Cap maximum interval at 5 minutes (300s) to ensure regular checks
- Reset interval if errors detected (back to frequent checking)

**When to use:**
- ✅ Long-running scripts (> 10 minutes expected)
- ✅ Scripts that process large datasets
- ✅ Background monitoring after initial validation
- ✅ Scripts where you want to reduce monitoring overhead over time

**When fixed interval is OK:**
- ✅ Short scripts (< 10 minutes)
- ✅ Critical operations requiring constant monitoring
- ✅ Initial validation phase (first 5-10 minutes)

**Rationale:**
- Early errors are most critical (catch configuration issues quickly)
- Stable scripts don't need constant checking
- Slow backoff ensures issues are still caught in reasonable time
- Prevents excessive monitoring overhead for long-running scripts

**❌ BAD - Fire and forget (no monitoring):**
```bash
# Start script and walk away - no way to detect issues early
bazel run //:process_data > /tmp/process_data.log 2>&1 &
# No monitoring, no validation, no early error detection
# May run for hours before discovering it failed or processed data incorrectly
```

**❌ BAD - Check only at the end:**
```bash
# Start script
bazel run //:import_data > /tmp/import.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!

# Wait for completion (could be hours)
wait $PID

# Only check logs after completion - too late to fix issues
tail /tmp/import.log
# If errors found, all the work was wasted
```

**What to monitor:**
- ✅ **Initial startup** - Does script start correctly? (first 10-30 seconds)
- ✅ **Progress indicators** - Are records being processed? (counts, percentages)
- ✅ **Error patterns** - Any exceptions, warnings, or failures?
- ✅ **Performance** - Is script progressing at expected rate?
- ✅ **Resource usage** - Is script consuming excessive CPU/memory?
- ✅ **Data validation** - Are results reasonable? (sample output, counts)

**When to intervene:**
- ❌ **Errors detected** - Stop immediately, investigate, fix, restart
- ❌ **No progress for 2+ minutes** - Script may be stuck (database lock, infinite loop)
- ❌ **Unexpected behavior** - Output doesn't match expectations
- ❌ **Performance issues** - Much slower than expected (may indicate problem)
- ❌ **Resource exhaustion** - High CPU/memory usage (may need optimization)

**Early course correction benefits:**
- ✅ Catch configuration errors before processing thousands of records
- ✅ Detect data issues before corrupting database
- ✅ Identify performance problems before script runs for hours
- ✅ Fix bugs before wasting compute resources
- ✅ Validate assumptions early (script works as expected)

**Rationale:**
- Prevents terminal blocking and improves responsiveness
- Enables early detection of stuck scripts (no output = likely stuck)
- Allows monitoring progress without interrupting execution
- Better debugging (logs persist even if terminal closes)
- Follows best practices for long-running operations
- **Critical addition:** Active monitoring and early intervention prevents wasted time and resources, catches issues before they compound, and enables course correction when it matters most

## Rule: Avoid Interactive Mode Issues in Background Commands

**CRITICAL: NEVER use complex quoting, variable expansion, conditionals, or multi-line logic in background commands that can cause the shell to enter interactive mode (waiting for input).**

**Why this matters:**
- Shell can get stuck waiting for closing quotes, brackets, or command completion
- Complex conditionals (if/then/else) in background commands can break parsing
- Multi-line commands with pipes and conditionals can cause shell to wait for input
- Background processes with parsing issues may hang indefinitely
- Interactive prompts (like `dquote>`, `pipe>`) block execution and prevent monitoring
- Simple, straightforward commands are more reliable

**❌ BAD - Complex quoting in background command:**
```bash
# ❌ BAD: Complex quoting with $! can cause shell to wait for input
bazel run //:script -- --output $(pwd)/data/file.jsonl > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &
echo "Started with PID: $!"
# Shell may get stuck waiting for closing quote or command completion
```

**❌ BAD - Multi-line background command with variable expansion:**
```bash
# ❌ BAD: Multi-line command with $! can cause interactive mode
bazel run //:script -- \
  --output $(pwd)/data/file.jsonl \
  > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &
echo "PID: $!"
# Shell may enter dquote> or > prompt waiting for input
```

**❌ BAD - Complex conditionals in background command:**
```bash
# ❌ BAD: Complex if/then/else with pipes can break parsing
bazel run //:script > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
if ps -p $PID > /dev/null 2>&1; then
  tail -20 /tmp/log.log | grep "Phase" | tail -5
else
  tail -30 /tmp/log.log
fi
# Shell may get stuck waiting for closing brackets or quotes
```

**✅ GOOD - Simple background command:**
```bash
# ✅ GOOD: Simple, single-line background command
bazel run //:script -- --output /path/to/data/file.jsonl > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID"
echo "Monitor with: tail -f /tmp/log.log"
```

**✅ GOOD - Separate PID capture:**
```bash
# ✅ GOOD: Run command, then capture PID separately
bazel run //:script -- --output /path/to/data/file.jsonl > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID"
```

**✅ GOOD - Using nohup for persistence:**
```bash
# ✅ GOOD: nohup handles background execution cleanly
nohup bazel run //:script -- --output /path/to/data/file.jsonl > /tmp/log.log 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID, monitoring: tail -f /tmp/log.log"
```

**✅ GOOD - Pre-expand variables before background:**
```bash
# ✅ GOOD: Expand variables before running in background
OUTPUT_FILE="/path/to/data/file.jsonl"
LOG_FILE="/tmp/log.log"
bazel run //:script -- --output "$OUTPUT_FILE" > "$LOG_FILE" 2>&1 &
PID=$!
echo "Started with PID: $PID"
```

**When to use simple patterns:**
- ✅ Always use simple, single-line background commands
- ✅ Capture PID separately after the `&` command
- ✅ Pre-expand variables before background execution
- ✅ Use absolute paths instead of `$(pwd)` in background commands
- ✅ Avoid complex quoting or multi-line commands with `&`
- ✅ Run status checks in separate commands (not in same line as background)
- ✅ Use simple monitoring commands: `tail -f /tmp/log.log` or `ps -p $PID`

**When to avoid:**
- ❌ Don't use `$!` in the same command line as the background process
- ❌ Don't use complex variable expansion in background commands
- ❌ Don't use multi-line commands ending with `&`
- ❌ Don't use nested command substitution like `$(pwd)` in background commands
- ❌ Don't use if/then/else conditionals in the same command as background execution
- ❌ Don't chain multiple pipes and conditionals in background command setup
- ❌ Don't use complex shell logic (loops, conditionals) in background command lines

**Rationale:**
- Simple commands are less likely to cause shell parsing issues
- Separating PID capture avoids quoting problems
- Pre-expanding variables makes commands more predictable
- Prevents shell from entering interactive mode waiting for input
- Makes monitoring and debugging easier
